ehavior, before proposing fixes</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/superpowers/systematic-debugging/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>test-driven-development</name>\n    <description>Use when implementing any feature or bugfix, before writing implementation code</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/superpowers/test-driven-development/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>using-git-worktrees</name>\n    <description>Use when starting feature work that needs isolation from current workspace or before executing implementation plans - creates isolated git worktrees with smart directory selection and safety verification</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/superpowers/using-git-worktrees/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>using-superpowers</name>\n    <description>Use when starting any conversation - establishes how to find and use skills, requiring Skill tool invocation before ANY response including clarifying questions</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/superpowers/using-superpowers/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>verification-before-completion</name>\n    <description>Use when about to claim work is complete, fixed, or passing, before committing or creating PRs - requires running verification commands and confirming output before making any success claims; evidence before assertions always</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/superpowers/verification-before-completion/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>writing-plans</name>\n    <description>Use when you have a spec or requirements for a multi-step task, before touching code</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/superpowers/writing-plans/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>writing-skills</name>\n    <description>Use when creating new skills, editing existing skills, or verifying skills work before deployment</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/superpowers/writing-skills/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>tdd</name>\n    <description>Test-driven development. Use when the user wants to build features or fix bugs test-first, mentions &quot;red-green-refactor&quot;, or wants integration tests.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/tdd/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>to-spec</name>\n    <description>Turn the current conversation into a spec and publish it to the project issue tracker: no interview, just synthesis of what you&apos;ve already discussed.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/to-spec/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>to-tickets</name>\n    <description>Break a plan, spec, or the current conversation into a set of tracer-bullet tickets, each declaring its blocking edges, published to the configured tracker (edges as text in one file per ticket locally, or native blocking links on a real tracker).</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/to-tickets/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>trycycle</name>\n    <description>Invoke trycycle only when the user requests it by name.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/trycycle/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>typeset</name>\n    <description>Improves typography by fixing font choices, hierarchy, sizing, weight, and readability so text feels intentional. Use when the user mentions fonts, type, readability, text hierarchy, sizing looks off, or wants more polished, intentional typography.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/typeset/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n</available_skills>\n</skills>","cwd":"<cwd>\n/Users/nick/code/roast.ts-rewrite-35-doctor\n</cwd>"},"timestamp":1795205182061,"toolsAdded":[{"name":"read","description":"Read the contents of a file. Supports text files and images (jpg, png, gif, webp, bmp). Images are sent as attachments. For text files, output is truncated to 2000 lines or 50KB (whichever is hit first). Use offset/limit for large files. When you need the full file, continue with offset until complete.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["path"],"properties":{"path":{"type":"string","description":"Path to the file to read (relative or absolute)"},"offset":{"type":"number","description":"Line number to start reading from (1-indexed)"},"limit":{"type":"number","description":"Maximum number of lines to read"}}},"constrainedSampling":{"type":"json_schema","strict":"prefer"}},{"name":"bash","description":"Execute a bash command in the current working directory. Returns stdout and stderr. Output is truncated to last 2000 lines or 50KB (whichever is hit first). If truncated, full output is saved to a temp file. Optionally provide a timeout in seconds.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["command"],"properties":{"command":{"type":"string","description":"Shell command to execute"},"timeout":{"type":"number","description":"Timeout in seconds (optional, no default timeout)"}}},"constrainedSampling":{"type":"json_schema","strict":"prefer"}},{"name":"edit","description":"Edit a single file using exact text replacement. Every edits[].oldText must match a unique, non-overlapping region of the original file. If two changes affect the same block or nearby lines, merge them into one edit instead of emitting overlapping edits. Do not include large unchanged regions just to connect distant changes.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["path","edits"],"properties":{"path":{"type":"string","description":"Path to the file to edit (relative or absolute)"},"edits":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","required":["oldText","newText"],"properties":{"oldText":{"type":"string","description":"Exact text for one targeted replacement. It must be unique in the original file and must not overlap with any other edits[].oldText in the same call."},"newText":{"type":"string","description":"Replacement text for this targeted edit."}}},"description":"One or more targeted replacements. Each edit is matched against the original file, not incrementally. Do not include overlapping or nested edits. If two changes touch the same block or nearby lines, merge them into one edit instead."}}},"constrainedSampling":{"type":"json_schema","strict":"prefer"}},{"name":"write","description":"Write content to a file. Creates the file if it doesn't exist, overwrites if it does. Automatically creates parent directories.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["path","content"],"properties":{"path":{"type":"string","description":"Path to the file to write (relative or absolute)"},"content":{"type":"string","description":"Content to write to the file"}}},"constrainedSampling":{"type":"json_schema","strict":"prefer"}},{"name":"live_context_annotate","description":"Create, resolve, or list branch-local continuity annotations. Create uses a current mirror block ID or active Pi entry ID. pin keeps a bounded exact textual source visible; continuity keeps a durable reason/next-action pointer; archive is recall-only.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["action"],"properties":{"action":{"type":"string","enum":["create","resolve","list"]},"source":{"type":"string","description":"Current CTX_TURN block ID or active-branch Pi entry ID"},"id":{"type":"string","description":"Annotation ID for resolve"},"title":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":120},"reason":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":400},"futureAction":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":400},"retention":{"type":"string","enum":["pin","continuity","archive"]},"resolution":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":400}}}},{"name":"live_context_recall","description":"Recall the exact textual source for a branch-local annotation by ID. Output is bounded to 128-8000 estimated tokens (default 2000) and validated against the durable Pi entry's content hash.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["id"],"properties":{"id":{"type":"string","description":"Durable live-context annotation ID"},"maxTokens":{"type":"integer","minimum":128,"maximum":8000}}}}]}}
{"type":"message_end","message":{"role":"system","content":"","sections":{"preamble":"You are a coding assistant running inside pi, a coding agent harness. Help users inspect files, run commands, and modify code.","tools":"<tools>\n- read: Read file contents\n- bash: Execute bash commands (ls, grep, find, etc.)\n- edit: Make precise file edits with exact text replacement, including multiple disjoint edits in one call\n- write: Create or overwrite files\n- live_context_annotate: Create, resolve, or list durable annotations for exact context sources\n- live_context_recall: Recall a bounded exact source from a durable live-context annotation\n\nAdditional tools may be available.\n</tools>","rules":"<rules>\n- Use bash for file operations like ls, rg, find\n- Use read to examine files instead of cat or sed.\n- You can inspect PI_* environment variables for current model and session details.\n- Use edit for targeted changes. Batch changes to one file in a single call.\n- Each edits[].oldText must exactly and uniquely match the original file. Keep matches short and non-overlapping; merge nearby changes without including large unchanged regions.\n- Use write only for new files or complete rewrites.\n- Use live_context_annotate before compacting an exact source that future work must preserve; choose pin only when its exact text must remain visible, continuity for a visible obligation and recall pointer, or archive for recall-only provenance.\n- Use live_context_recall only when an active continuity pointer or archived source is needed; request the smallest useful maxTokens bound.\n- Be concise in your responses\n- Show file paths clearly when working with files\n</rules>","docs":"<docs>\nPi documentation (read only when the user asks about the harness itself, its SDK, extensions, themes, skills, or TUI):\n- Main documentation: /Users/nick/.pi/agent/install/releases/1.5.2/node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/README.md\n- Additional docs: /Users/nick/.pi/agent/install/releases/1.5.2/node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/docs\n- Examples: /Users/nick/.pi/agent/install/releases/1.5.2/node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/examples (extensions, custom tools, SDK)\n- When reading pi docs or examples, resolve docs/... under Additional docs and examples/... under Examples, not the current working directory\n- When asked about: extensions (docs/extensions.md, examples/extensions/), themes (docs/themes.md), skills (docs/skills.md), prompt templates (docs/prompt-templates.md), TUI components (docs/tui.md), keybindings (docs/keybindings.md), SDK integrations (docs/sdk.md), custom providers (docs/custom-provider.md), adding models (docs/models.md), packages (docs/packages.md), environment variables (docs/environment-variables.md), MCP servers (docs/mcp.md), codemode scripts and non-LLM models such as classifiers and image models (docs/codemode.md)\n- Before implementing harness changes, read the relevant docs and examples in full, following related cross-references.\n</docs>","project_context":"<project_context>\nProject-specific instructions and guidelines:\n\n<project_instructions path=\"/Users/nick/code/roast.ts-rewrite-35-doctor/AGENTS.md\">\n<!-- redeye:start -->\n## Agent skills\n\n### Issue tracker\n\nIssues are tracked as local markdown files under `.scratch/`. See `docs/agents/issue-tracker.md`.\n\n### Triage labels\n\nDefault label vocabulary. See `docs/agents/triage-labels.md`.\n\n### Domain docs\n\nSingle-context layout. See `docs/agents/domain.md`.\n<!-- redeye:end -->\n\n## Go conventions\n\n- CI runs `go mod tidy` and fails on a diff, so run it after removing code\n  that was the last user of a dependency.\n- CI runs staticcheck (`go tool -modfile=.github/tools/staticcheck/go.mod\n  staticcheck ./...`); deleting a caller can leave an unused helper that\n  fails U1000.\n- `scripts/licenses.sh` special-cases notices for specific linked packages.\n  When a dependency drops out of the `./cmd/roast` graph, check\n  `go list -deps ./cmd/roast` for each target and update the script.\n- CLI tests drive `ExecuteContext` against an `httptest` fake inference server;\n  `internal/testutil.ClearAmbientEnvironment` strips `ROAST_*`, `OPENAI_*`,\n  `OPENROUTER_*` and `ANTHROPIC_*` in `TestMain`. Config tests reset single\n  variables with `clearProviderEnv` (`internal/config/credential_test.go`);\n  add a new provider's key variable there too.\n## Project config (`internal/config`, `internal/cli/config.go`)\n\n- `config.Load(path)` parses `.roast.json` as JSONC (`hujson.Standardize`,\n  then `encoding/json` with `DisallowUnknownFields`). `version: 1` is required;\n  a literal `api_key` is rejected. There are no built-in roles or models: a\n  role missing from the config fails in `ResolveRole`.\n- Built-in providers (`internal/config/provider.go`): `LookupProvider(name)`\n  and `ProviderAt(endpoint, protocol)` are the one place that says which\n  endpoint, protocols, standard key variable, key page, and login method a\n  provider has. Trust standardness, ambient credentials, and the `init`\n  starter all consult it; add a provider there, not in those callers. A\n  provider's first protocol is the one starter profiles use.\n  `DefaultBaseURL(protocol)` stays separate: it is the implicit endpoint of\n  a profile that omits one; OpenRouter is never one. A role that gets no\n  ambient key reports any set provider key variable as `Withheld`\n  (`withheldKey`), preferring the protocol's default provider.\n- `config.Find(start, stop)` walks up to `stop` (the Git toplevel). It compares\n  directories after `EvalSymlinks` because macOS temp dirs are `/var/...` while\n  `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` prints `/private/var/...`.\n- The CLI loads config only through `loadProjectConfig` / `projectConfigPath`\n  in `internal/cli/config.go`; a missing config is a usage error naming\n  `npx roast init`.\n- CLI tests that run a review need a `.roast.json` (see\n  `internal/cli/project_config_test.go` helpers `projectConfig`, `writeFile`,\n  `chdir`, `newModelRecorder`). Profiles in test configs name a credential\n  with `api_key_env` and set it via `t.Setenv`.\n- Only credentials come from outside the config (`ROAST_<ROLE>_API_KEY`, a\n  profile's `api_key_env`, then `OPENAI_API_KEY`/`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`, then\n  stored credentials, the last three only at that provider's built-in\n  endpoint). Every other setting is in the config\n  (`limits`, `interactive`, `gate`, `review.snapshot`); optional numbers are\n  pointers so an explicit 0 differs from unset, and `validateSettings` checks\n  ranges. In `cli.go` resolve with `flagOrConfigInt` /\n  `flagOrConfigDuration` (flag, then config, then default).\n- Relative prompt-override paths resolve against the config file's\n  directory: `config.Load` records it and callers read prompts through\n  `cfg.PromptPath(...)`, never the raw `cfg.Prompts.*` string. The trust\n  projection hashes the raw string, so approvals survive worktree moves.\n- Untracked files are part of a review's diff, so a test that checks which\n  file's text reached the fake server must keep decoys out of scope (for\n  example via `.git/info/exclude`).\n- `roast.schema.json` at the repo root is the editor schema for\n  `.roast.json`, shipped through `package.json` `files`. Adding, removing,\n  or retyping a `config.Config` field must update it in the same change;\n  `TestSchemaMatchesConfigStruct` (`internal/config/schema_test.go`) fails\n  on drift either way. The `roast init` starter\n  (`internal/config/starter.go`) shows commented-out defaults that must\n  track the defaults in `internal/cli/cli.go`.\n- The starter places a model by its ID alone (`placeModel`): a\n  `<vendor>/<model>[:<variant>]` ID goes to OpenRouter, a bare ID to the\n  provider `InferFamily` names. A placeholder role follows an OpenRouter\n  role onto OpenRouter; otherwise it takes the other direct provider.\n- `internal/cli/init_test.go` has `providerServer`, a fake speaking the\n  Responses (`/v1/responses`), Anthropic (`/v1/messages`), and OpenRouter\n  chat (`/api/v1/chat/completions`) protocols;\n  pair it with `redirectDefaultTransport(t, server.URL)` to serve profiles\n  on the providers' default endpoints.\n- `internal/cli/settings_test.go` has `requestRecorder` (records model,\n  `Authorization`, body), `settingsRepo`, `reviewMeta` (reads the run's\n  `meta.json`), and `redirectDefaultTransport`, which swaps\n  `http.DefaultTransport` so a profile on a provider's default endpoint can\n  be served by the fake server.\n\n## Trust (`internal/config/trust.go`, `internal/trust`, `internal/cli/trust.go`)\n\n- `Config.TrustProjection()` is the security-relevant projection (ADR-0001);\n  its `Hash()` covers only endpoint, protocol, credential variable, and\n  prompt-override paths. Adding a security-relevant config field means\n  adding it to the projection, or it can change without re-approval.\n- Approvals live in `<git-common-dir>/roast/trust.json`. `roast review`\n  calls `requireTrust` before building scope; untrusted is a usage error\n  (exit 2), never `ExitFindings`.\n- Any CLI test that reviews against the `httptest` server uses a custom\n  endpoint, so it must approve first: `approveProjectConfig(t, ...)` in\n  `trust_test.go` (runs `trust --yes`), or `settingsRepo`, which approves.\n  For a test asserting a config error, approve a valid config and then edit\n  it (`approvedThenEdited`) so a trust failure cannot mask the error.\n- `executeContext(ctx, args, stdin, stdout, stderr)` lets tests answer\n  prompts; `ExecuteContext` passes `os.Stdin`.\n- Terminal detection goes through the package var `interactiveTerminals`\n  (`internal/cli/prompt.go`); tests swap it with `simulateTerminals(t, ...)`\n  (`trust_prompt_test.go`) to exercise the inline trust prompt in `review`.\n- `roast doctor` skips endpoint probes for an unapproved config. A doctor\n  test that probes a custom endpoint must chdir into a temp repo and approve\n  (`approveDoctorConfig` in `doctor_test.go`), never approve in this\n  checkout.\n\n## Stored credentials (`internal/credentials`)\n\n- Project scope is `<git-common-dir>/roast/credentials.json`\n  (`trust.StateDir`, shared with `trust.json`); user scope is\n  `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/roast/credentials.json`, else\n  `~/.config/roast/credentials.json` (ADR-0002).\n- `config` cannot import `credentials` (it validates provider names through\n  `config.LookupProvider`), so `config.StoredCredentials` is an interface.\n  The CLI attaches `credentials.NewStore(\".\")` with `withStoredCredentials`\n  in `internal/cli/config.go`; any new path that resolves roles from a\n  `config.Load` result must attach it too, or stored keys are silently\n  unused. `ResolvedRole` carries the store so `OverrideEndpoint` can rebind.\n- Initial resolution (`resolveCredential`) and override rebinding\n  (`rebindCredential`) must agree: both reach the provider variable and\n  stored key through `ambientCredential`, so a role picks the same\n  credential with or without a same-provider protocol override. Keep\n  `DescribeCredential` in step with any change to that order.\n- Files are read lazily: a bad file fails resolution only when a role\n  reaches it (no env credential first). `roast doctor` also loads each\n  scope's file that exists (`doctorStoredCredentialChecks`, results\n  `project-credentials` / `user-credentials`), so a broken file fails\n  doctor even when env credentials shadow it. Doctor reads the user-scope\n  file too, so doctor tests that expect a clean exit should isolate `HOME`.\n- Tests that touch stored credentials chdir into a temp Git repository (the\n  project file lands in its `.git`) and isolate `HOME` and\n  `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` (`isolateCredentialHome` in\n  `internal/cli/stored_credentials_test.go`).\n\n## Login (`internal/cli/login.go`)\n\n- Browser opening goes through the package var `openBrowser`\n  (`internal/cli/prompt.go`, takes a URL). Tests never launch a browser:\n  swap it with `captureBrowser(t)` (`login_test.go`), which returns the\n  URLs it was asked to open. Login prints every URL too, so a failing\n  opener never blocks it.\n- `readSecret` reads without echo only on a real terminal; with test\n  stdin it reads plain lines from one `bufio.Reader` kept across retries,\n  so tests pass several answers as `\"key1\\nkey2\\n\"`.\n- `providerServer` (`init_test.go`) answers `GET /v1/models` for key\n  validation; `answerModels` sets the status per credential. With\n  `redirectDefaultTransport`, login's validation request reaches it.\n  `loginRepo(t)` in `login_test.go` sets up repo, fake, terminal, and\n  opener in one call.\n- `login` with no argument and `init`'s login offer share\n  `providersNeedingLogin` (built-in providers of roles that resolve no\n  credential) and `loginProvider` (one provider, given scope path). A\n  command that prompts and then reads keys must pass one `*bufio.Reader`\n  to both `promptYesDefault` and `loginProvider`; a fresh reader per\n  prompt swallows the buffered answers that tests feed as one string.\n  `initLoginRepo(t, terminals)` in `init_test.go` sets up init tests.\n- OpenRouter login (`openrouter_login.go`) is a PKCE exchange: the fake\n  mints `sk-or-minted-<code>` at `POST /api/v1/auth/keys` and checks keys\n  at `GET /api/v1/key` (`answerKey`). Tests run login in the background\n  (`startRoast`), read the opened URL (`awaitOpened`), and hit its\n  `callback_url` with `callOpenRouterBack`, which uses its own transport\n  because `redirectDefaultTransport` would send loopback requests to the\n  fake. Shorten the callback wait with `shortBrowserTimeout`; any test\n  that might wait for a callback should, so a regression fails fast\n  instead of hanging for five minutes.\n- `logout` (`internal/cli/logout.go`) shares `loginPath` (scope and the\n  outside-a-repository usage error) and `providerNames` with login; keep\n  scope rules in that one helper. Tests seed several providers with\n  `storeKeys` (`logout_test.go`, wraps `credentials.Save`).\n- `storedCredentialRepo` swaps `http.DefaultTransport`, so call it at most\n  once per test or subtest; loop over cases with `t.Run`.\n\n## Agent skill install (`internal/skill`, `internal/cli/skill.go`)\n\n- `skill.InstallationTargets(parent, projectRoot)` resolves targets: a\n  non-empty `parent` (`--dir`) wins, then a non-empty `projectRoot` gives\n  project-local targets, otherwise the user-level targets.\n  `internal/cli/skill.go` maps `--dir`, `--global`, and the Git toplevel onto\n  it; `doctor` inspects user-level targets.\n- The bundled skill text in `internal/skill/assets/roast/SKILL.md` describes\n  the install locations; update it when target resolution changes.\n- CLI skill tests isolate `HOME`, `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR`, and `XDG_CONFIG_HOME`\n  (see `isolateSkillHome` in `internal/cli/skill_test.go`) so they can assert\n  nothing is written to the real user directories. Do not clear `PATH`: the\n  CLI shells out to `git`.\n## Run records (`internal/record`)\n\n- `record.DefaultDir()` is `.roast/runs` at the Git toplevel of the process\n  working directory (`\"\"` outside a repository); there is no env override.\n  Run directories are `<DefaultDir>/<run-id>/`, the monthly JSONL index is\n  `<DefaultDir>/runs-YYYY-MM.jsonl`.\n- Any test that writes or reads records must `t.Chdir` into a temporary Git\n  repository first (see `useProject` in `internal/record/record_test.go`).\n  Otherwise records land in this checkout's `.roast/`, which ignores itself,\n  so `git status` will not reveal the leak; check with `ls -a`.\n- `roast ignore` (`internal/cli/ignore.go`, `ensureStateIgnored`) owns the\n  toplevel `/.roast/` entry; reuse it rather than writing `.gitignore` again.\n\n## Gate (`internal/gate`, `internal/cli/gate.go`)\n\n- The hook (`internal/gate/pre-commit.sh`, embedded as `HookScript`) runs\n  only `node_modules/.bin/roast` at the Git toplevel, never `PATH`. It first\n  calls the hidden `roast gate settings` (prints `timeout_seconds=` and\n  `severity=` lines from the config's `gate` section, defaults 180/HIGH),\n  then `roast review --staged ... --max-severity <severity>`. Any failure,\n  including a settings query that exits non-zero, warns and allows the\n  commit; only review exit 1 blocks.\n- The hook runs from the Git toplevel without `--config`, so it sees only a\n  toplevel `.roast.json` (`gateConfigPath` in `internal/cli/config.go`).\n  `gate enable` refuses and the review-time bootstrap stays silent without\n  one; `gate enable` tests must write a toplevel config first.\n- Installed hooks are refreshed by content comparison with `HookScript`, so\n  editing the script needs no version bump.\n- Hook execution tests (`hook_exec_test.go`) drive a real `git commit` with\n  a shell stub that `newStub(t, repo...)` writes at each repo's\n  `node_modules/.bin/roast`; it answers `gate settings` from `STUB_TIMEOUT` /\n  `STUB_SEVERITY` and logs only the review call (`ARGS:` plus the staged\n  diff), so `stub.ran` is empty when no review happened.\n\n## npm packaging (`src/launcher.ts`, `scripts/build.ts`, `packages/`)\n\n- The root `bin` is `dist/launcher.js`, compiled from `src/launcher.ts` by\n  `npm run build:launcher` (`tsc`). It runs\n  `@substrate-system/roast-<platform>-<arch>/dist/roast[.exe]`; the six\n  platform packages live in `packages/` as npm workspaces.\n- `npm run build -- --host` builds only this machine's binary plus the\n  launcher. `npx roast` in this repo resolves the root package's own bin,\n  so build first; a stale or non-executable `dist/launcher.js` lets a\n  `roast` on `PATH` (for example Homebrew) answer instead.\n- `.npmrc` sets `force=true`: npm refuses (EBADPLATFORM) to link workspaces\n  whose `os`/`cpu` do not match the host, even as optional dependencies.\n  Expect \"using --force\" warnings from every npm command here.\n- Launcher tests (`test/*.test.ts`, `node --test`) spawn the compiled\n  launcher from a temp fixture `node_modules` with a shell-script binary,\n  so they need `npm run build:launcher` first; `npm run test:go` does this.\n- Build scripts are TypeScript run directly by Node (type stripping), so\n  they may use only erasable syntax: no enums, namespaces, or parameter\n  properties.\n- `scripts/platforms.ts` is the single list of the six targets and their\n  package names/paths; `build.ts`, `version.ts` (`version` lifecycle),\n  `check-release.ts` (`prepublishOnly`) and `release.ts` share it. All but\n  `build.ts` operate on `process.cwd()` so tests can run them in a fixture.\n- Release-script tests (`test/release-fixture.ts`) build a temp package\n  with the real root `scripts` and a symlink to `scripts/`, then run\n  `npm version` / `npm run prepublishOnly` there; `release.test.ts` puts a\n  logging fake `npm` first on `PATH`. None of them compile Go.\n- `npm run check:pack` (`scripts/check-pack.ts`, CI only) dry-run packs\n  every package and checks each tarball lists its `dist/` contents; changing\n  a package's `files` or what `build.ts` writes into `dist/` must keep it\n  passing.\n  It also requires Roast's own license files (`LICENSES` in\n  `scripts/platforms.ts`, copied into each platform `dist/` by `build.ts`)\n  and a `license` field in every manifest.\n- `npm run release` runs `scripts/check-release.ts` itself, with Node, after\n  the build and before `npm publish --workspaces`: platform packages have no\n  `prepublishOnly`, so the root hook alone would run too late.\n- The root package has no `dependencies`; the launcher uses only Node\n  built-ins, and anything listed there installs into every user's project. npm 12 prints `npm pack --json` as an object keyed by package\n  name, older npm as an array.\n- There is no committed lockfile (`.npmrc` sets `package-lock=false`). CI\n  writes one with `npm install --package-lock-only --package-lock=true`\n  before `npm ci`, which refuses to run without it.\n- CI's test step runs `npm run test:go` with `GOFLAGS=-race`, so Go tests that\n  spawn `go` subprocesses inherit `-race`; such a subprocess must not also\n  set `CGO_ENABLED=0` for a build.\n- A full `npm run build` takes several minutes: it runs\n  `scripts/licenses.sh` once per target and copies the resulting root\n  `THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES/` into each package's `dist/`.\n\n## TypeScript rewrite tooling (M0 to M5)\n\n- `npm run test:ts` bundles `dist/cli.js` (`npm run bundle`), bundles\n  `test/index.ts` to `test/test-bundle.js` (`build:test`), and pipes it to\n  `tap-spec` (`test:tap`). A new test module must be imported from\n  `test/index.ts`. `npm test` runs `typecheck` and then this TS suite\n  (`test:ts` is an alias); esbuild drops unused imports without\n  resolving them, so only typecheck catches a broken import.\n  `npm run test:go` runs the Go and launcher tests.\n- `scripts/bundle.ts` holds the esbuild options for every bundle (text\n  loaders for `.tmpl`, `.md`, `.sh`; `mainFields: ['module', 'main']`;\n  the `require` shim banner). A test that bundles code itself must use\n  the same loaders and banner.\n- Lint runs eslint through `scripts/eslint-ts6.ts`, a resolve hook that\n  gives the eslint process the TypeScript 6 API\n  (`@typescript/typescript6`), because typescript-eslint throws on the\n  TypeScript 7 package that `typecheck` uses. Run lint with\n  `npm run lint`, not a bare `eslint`.\n- Legacy launcher and release files are excluded from lint and typecheck\n  by name (`lint` `--ignore-pattern` list, `tsconfig.json` `exclude`); the\n  launcher compiles with `tsconfig.launcher.json`.\n- Startup guards live in `src/startup.ts`, not `src/cli.ts`: importing\n  the entry runs the CLI, so testable helpers must sit in other modules.\n- `npm run bundle` also writes `dist/THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.txt` through\n  `scripts/licenses.ts` and fails, naming the package, when a bundled\n  package lacks an allowlisted `license` field or a license file. A new\n  runtime dependency must pass that check; extending\n  `ALLOWED_LICENSES` needs maintainer approval.\n  `@substrate-system/debug` currently fails it, so no `src/` module may\n  import it yet.\n- `scripts/check-package.ts` is the post-M6 package check. It fails\n  against today's transitional manifest, so only its fixture tests\n  (`test/tooling/check-package.ts`) run it; do not wire it into CI or\n  npm scripts before M6.\n- Tests that spawn processes use `test/helpers/spawn.ts` (`ROOT`, `run`).\n  Shell hooks here block heredocs that mention `process.env.PATH`; write\n  such files with an editor tool.\n\n- Shared TS helpers (import these, never re-implement): `src/gocompat/`\n  (`strings.ts`: Go TrimSpace/Ext/`%.2f`/strconv.Quote/byte-order sort and\n  `truncateUtf8`; `duration.ts`: Go durations as bigint nanoseconds;\n  `match.ts`: Go `path.Match`), `src/textsafe/textsafe.ts`,\n  `src/fsutil/atomic.ts` (`writeFileAtomic`, options cover trust,\n  credentials, and gate temp names and error messages), `src/git/git.ts`\n  (the only git runner; pass the CLI context's `env`, the runner reads\n  `process.env` only when none is given), `src/util/pool.ts` (`mapPool`).\n- Git runner tests observe a child by putting a fake `git` shell script\n  first on the injected `PATH` (spawn resolves the command through\n  `env.PATH`); see `test/git/git.ts`.\n- TS CLI core (`src/cli/`): commands are `CommandDef`s\n  (`src/cli/command.ts`) listed in `src/cli/commands.ts`; `run.ts`\n  builds the yargs tree from that list plus `registerCommand` (tests),\n  and calls the handler only after parsing succeeds. A handler gets a\n  `RunContext` (cwd, env, signal, fetch, one shared `LineReader`) and\n  throws `UsageError` / `ExitSignal` / any error; never writes it or\n  reads process globals. A group with `subcommands` and no handler\n  prints its help.\n- Terminal detection and browser opening are swapped through\n  `terminalHooks` (`src/cli/terminal.ts`); tests use `simulateTerminals`\n  / `captureBrowser` (`test/helpers/terminal.ts`) with the `test`\n  wrapper in `test/helpers/test.ts`, which adds Go-style `t.cleanup`\n  (tapzero has no teardown hook).\n- CLI tests run in-process with `runRoast` (`test/helpers/run.ts`); it\n  makes a temp repo with an isolated env (`test/helpers/repo.ts`:\n  `tempRepo`, `tempDir`, which also sets `GIT_CEILING_DIRECTORIES`).\n  `test/cli/process.ts` spawns `dist/cli.js`, so it needs a fresh\n  `npm run bundle` (test:ts does that).\n- With only the default `$0` command registered, yargs omits the\n  Commands section from root help; it appears once a second command\n  registers.\n- eslint here rejects control characters in regex literals\n  (`no-control-regex`) and labeled statements (`no-labels`); scan char\n  codes and use helper functions instead.\n- Keep non-ASCII out of TS source: write `\\uXXXX` escapes. Some editing\n  tools decode such escapes into literal characters when writing; check\n  with `grep -nP '[^\\x00-\\x7f]'` after writing a file.\n- Scope (`src/scope/`, public surface in `index.ts`): every `git diff`\n  argument list starts with `DIFF_CONFIG` (use `diffArgs(...)` in\n  `src/scope/git.ts`); git rejects an empty `diff.orderFile=`, so it is\n  `/dev/null`. Patch output goes through `gitPatch` (streams into a\n  `SectionCapper`), never the buffered runner. `build` takes the git\n  environment as `BuildOpts.env` (the CLI context's env).\n- The secret denylist lives once in `src/snapshot/denylist.ts`; scope,\n  context, and snapshot import it.\n- Scope tests use `newRepo()` (`test/scope/helpers.ts`): a temp repo with\n  its own HOME, XDG_CONFIG_HOME, and `GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL` file\n  (`repo.globalConfig`, safe to fill with hostile config), passed to\n  `build` as `env:repo.env`. APFS refuses non-UTF-8 file names; stage such\n  a name with `git update-index --index-info` to test it on macOS.\n- Memory bounds: plain RSS growth reflects V8 GC timing on discarded pipe\n  chunks, not what code keeps alive. `test/scope/huge.ts` forces gc\n  (`v8.setFlagsFromString('--expose-gc')`, `vm.runInNewContext('gc')`)\n  and bounds `heapUsed + external`.\n- Shell hooks here also block heredocs and commands containing `env:` /\n  `.env` text; write such files with an editor tool.\n- Module-level swappable functions use a mutable hooks object plus a\n  delegating export (`terminalHooks`, `skillHooks.lookPath` in\n  `src/skill/skill.ts`), because tests cannot reassign an ESM `let`\n  export. Swap the object's field and restore it in `t.cleanup`.\n- TS skill (`src/skill/`): `src/skill/assets.ts` imports each file under\n  `internal/skill/assets/roast/` explicitly; adding a file there needs a\n  new import, and `test/skill/assets.ts` fails until it has one. Home,\n  `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR`, `XDG_CONFIG_HOME`, and `PATH` come from the\n  context env. CLI skill tests (`test/cli/skill.ts`, `isolateSkillHome`)\n  blank the two config overrides, because `isolatedEnv` sets\n  `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR`, which counts as Claude Code being present.\n- tapzero's `Test` has no `notOk`; write `t.ok(!x, ...)`.\n- LLM layer (`src/llm/`): adapters read no environment variable. A\n  protocol is an entry in `ADAPTERS` (`client.ts`) with a pure encoder\n  and an `AsyncIterable<SseEvent>` decoder returning a `ModelTurn`;\n  `send` owns fetch, headers (`http.ts` `requestHeaders`), capture, usage\n  and abort. One `errors.ts`: `LlmError.kind` (underscore names, read by\n  `src/cli/errors.ts`) and `sentinels` (hyphen names, `hasSentinel`) must\n  stay in step; tool results sent to models go through\n  `encodeToolResult` (`json.ts`) only.\n- LLM tests: `test/helpers/inference-server.ts` (`startInferenceServer`,\n  a script picks a reply per request; it records method, path, headers,\n  raw and parsed body) and `inference-stream.ts`\n  (`writeInferenceStream`, the native SSE per protocol). Byte-level body\n  behavior (read-ahead, caps, cancel counts) is tested with a\n  `highWaterMark: 0` `ReadableStream` returned through\n  `ClientConfig.fetch`; `new Response(stream)` neither copies nor\n  pre-reads it.\n- Agent loop (`src/llm/agent.ts`, `runConversation`): `createClient(\n  modelClient, {ledger, now, sleep, sessionId})` (`client.ts`) wraps a\n  `ModelClient` into the finder/verifier `Client`. The pure decision is\n  `reviewResponse` (`review.ts`); the deadline, ledger, final-argument\n  validation, and retries are separate modules. `ModelClient.send` owns\n  the request timeout; the loop composes only caller + deadline/window\n  signals. Cutoff errors come from `cutoffError` (`errors.ts`), which\n  keeps the cause out of the matchable chain; tests assert\n  `details.stopCause` kinds, never message text.\n- Agent-loop tests drive `harness(script)` from `test/llm/agent-helpers.ts`\n  (a scripted fake `ModelClient`: canned turns, errors, or functions of\n  the request; sleeps recorded). `AbortSignal.timeout` timers do not keep\n  Node alive, so a fake step that waits for an abort must hold a timer\n  (the `aborted()` helper does); otherwise the test process just exits.\n- Differential harness (`test/diff/`, temporary, deleted at M6):\n  `npm run test:diff` bundles the CLI and `test/diff/index.ts`, runs the\n  harness self-tests (no Go needed), then every scenario against the Go\n  baseline built at `cd08f10` into\n  `node_modules/.cache/roast-diff/` (delete it to force a rebuild).\n  Without `go` on `PATH` every scenario is a TAP skip. Add a milestone's\n  scenarios as a `Scenario[]` under `test/diff/scenarios/` and list it in\n  `SCENARIOS` (`index.ts`); `server`, `records`, and `requests` turn on\n  the fake server and record/request comparison. A difference caused by\n  a deliberate change goes in `ALLOWLIST` (`allowlist.ts`) naming the\n  change number; Go behavior judged wrong goes in the owning area's Open\n  questions, never patched in the harness.\n- Live tests live under `test/live/` (entry `index.ts`, `npm run\n  test:live`); each passes with a \"skipped\" note when its\n  `ROAST_LIVE_*` key is unset, so the TAP stream is never empty.\n- Context (`src/context/`): every function that runs git or reads files\n  takes a trailing `ContextOpts` (`signal`, `env`); pass the CLI\n  context's env, and the repo env in tests (`newRepo()` from\n  `test/scope/helpers.ts`). `pinIndexTree` reads `GIT_INDEX_FILE` from\n  that env, not `process.env`. Blocking git calls use the shared runner;\n  streamed reads use `gitStream` / `gitCapped` (`src/context/git.ts`).\n  Grounding and normalization work on `Buffer`s; decode to text only in\n  `snippet.ts` (model-facing). Abort tests put a pid-recording fake `git`\n  first on the env's `PATH` (`wrapGit` in `test/context/bounds.ts`).\n- Exploration tools (`src/context/tools.ts`): `explorationTools(repo,\n  tree, {env})`; each `run(args, signal)` returns a plain object, a\n  model-caused failure is `{error}`, abort rejects. Tests call tools\n  through `call(repo, tree, name, args)` and fake git with\n  `fakeGit`/`onArg` (`test/context/tools-helpers.ts`). When stopping a\n  `gitStream` early, `await lineReader.close()` before awaiting `done`:\n  a paused stdout keeps the child's `close` from firing.\n- Captured-worktree tests build the tree with plumbing (temporary\n  `GIT_INDEX_FILE`, `read-tree HEAD`, `add`, `write-tree`); calling\n  `write-tree` or `status` on the live index rewrites it, so snapshot\n  the index bytes after those calls when asserting it is unchanged.\n- TS config (`src/config/`): `jsonc.ts` parses into null-prototype\n  objects (`newObject`/`setKey`, so `__proto__` is an ordinary key);\n  `schema.ts` (zod) checks shape and types only, and `load.ts` keeps the\n  validated *input*, never zod's output (zod's record output turns a\n  `__proto__` key into a prototype). Range and cross-field checks are\n  plain functions in `load.ts` so Go's order holds. Editor-only\n  constraints (enum, pattern, minimum, required) go in `.meta()`.\n- `roast.schema.json` is generated: `npm run schema` (`scripts/schema.ts`,\n  run first by `bundle` and `build`) renders\n  `src/config/json-schema.ts`; `test/config/schema.ts` fails on drift.\n  Edit the zod schema, never the JSON file. Shared esbuild options live\n  in `scripts/esbuild-options.ts` (`bundle.ts` runs on import).\n- A one-off esbuild bundle of TS sources that pulls in `jsonc-parser`\n  needs `mainFields: ['module', 'main']` (its UMD build does dynamic\n  `require`). Configs the tests load as realistic examples go in\n  `test/config/examples.ts`; the schema test validates each with ajv.\n- The CLI loads config only through `loadProjectConfig(explicit, ctx)`\n  (`src/cli/config.ts`), which attaches `openCredentialStore(ctx.cwd,\n  ctx.env)` to every config it returns.\n- TS stored credentials (`src/credentials/credentials.ts`):\n  `CredentialStore.lookup` is synchronous (the config area resolves\n  roles synchronously), so the async `openCredentialStore` resolves both\n  scope paths up front and lookup reads files lazily with sync `fs`,\n  caching only successful loads. Decode with the strict zod schema but\n  read the validated input, as `config/load.ts` does. Tests put files at\n  `<repo>/.git/roast/credentials.json` and\n  `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/roast/credentials.json` from `tempRepo().env`.\n- A shell hook here blocks Bash commands whose text names a\n  `credentials*` path together with `cat`, `sed`, `head`, and the like;\n  read and edit those files with the editor tools.\n- TS gate (`src/gate/`): `HOOK_SCRIPT` is `internal/gate/pre-commit.sh`\n  via the text loader (byte-identical, so Go-installed hooks are not\n  stale); `hook.ts` handles hook bodies as `Buffer`s so strip keeps every\n  foreign byte. Hook writes use `fsutil` `writeFileAtomic` with `mode`.\n  Gate tests build repos with `gateRepo(t)` (`test/gate/helpers.ts`:\n  global/system git config cut out, maintenance off, toplevel\n  `.roast.json`); hook execution tests use `seededRepo`, `newStub`, and\n  `commit` from `test/gate/exec-helpers.ts`. Simulate a non-local\n  `roast.gate` with `GIT_CONFIG_COUNT`/`KEY_0`/`VALUE_0`, never a global\n  file. `ROAST_TEST_SKIP_GATE_TIMEOUT=1` skips the sleeping tests.\n- TS roles (`src/config/role.ts`, `credential.ts`, `provider.ts`):\n  resolution takes `env` explicitly and never reads `process.env`.\n  `ResolvedRole` is frozen with a non-enumerable `apiKey` and `stored`, so\n  JSON, spread, and `util.inspect` omit the secret; change a role with\n  `updateRole(role, changes)`, never `{...role}` (the spread drops the\n  key). `StoredCredentials` lives in `credential.ts` (re-exported by\n  `load.ts`). `provider.ts` `parseEndpoint` is the one endpoint parser\n  (treats `https:///v1` and `https:v1` as an empty host, like Go);\n  `endpoint.ts` and `sameEndpoint` use it.\n- Role tests build configs with `configFrom` / `resolveFinder` and the\n  in-memory `FakeStore` in `test/config/helpers.ts`.\n- TS trust: `src/config/trust.ts` (projection, `requiresApproval`, hash\n  v2) is pure; `src/config/canonical.ts` is the only JSON canonicalizer\n  (its input types must be `type` aliases, not interfaces, to be\n  assignable to `CanonicalValue`). `src/trust/trust.ts` takes a trailing\n  `{signal, env}` for git. `src/cli/trust.ts` has the command,\n  `writeTrustSummary` (every value through `textsafe.line`) and\n  `requireTrust(dir, cfg, io)`; review must call it before scope.\n  `promptNoDefault` (`[y/N]`) lives in `src/cli/terminal.ts`.\n- eslint `--fix` (quote-props) rewrites a quoted `'\\u00e9'` object key\n  into a literal non-ASCII identifier; put such keys in a const and use a\n  computed key.\n\n## Docs\n\n- User-facing docs (`README.md`, `docs/`, the bundled\n  `internal/skill/assets/roast/SKILL.md`) write every invocation as\n  `npx roast ...`; Roast is a project dependency, never a `PATH` binary.\n  Settings are documented as `.roast.json` keys, never environment\n  variables; only credentials, `ROAST_SKIP`, and test-only `ROAST_LIVE_*` /\n  `ROAST_TEST_*` variables exist.\n- CLI messages in `internal/cli` (for example the gate hint printed during\n  review) still say bare `roast`; changing them is a code change, not a docs\n  change.\n\n</project_instructions>\n</project_context>","skills":"<skills>\nThe following skills provide specialized instructions for specific tasks.\nUse the read tool to load a skill's file when the task matches its description.\nResolve relative paths against the skill's directory (parent of SKILL.md). Use absolute paths in tool calls.\n\n<available_skills>\n  <skill>\n    <name>amazon-bedrock</name>\n    <description>Builds generative AI applications on Amazon Bedrock. Covers model invocation (Converse API, InvokeModel), RAG with Knowledge Bases, Bedrock Agents, Guardrails, and AgentCore (including the Harness managed agent loop). Applies when invoking models, setting up Knowledge Bases, creating agents, applying guardrails, deploying to AgentCore, migrating/porting/converting a Bedrock Agent (including inline agents) to an AgentCore Harness, troubleshooting Bedrock errors (ThrottlingException, AccessDeniedException), or choosing models (Claude, Llama, Nova, Titan). Also for prompt caching, quota and throttling diagnosis, cost tracking, migrating between Claude model generations (4.5 to 4.6 to 4.7), chunking strategies, API selection (Converse vs InvokeModel), guardrail capabilities, and model selection. Also covers AgentCore Payments (x402, microtransactions, Payment Manager, Connector, Instrument, Coinbase CDP, Stripe Privy, paid endpoints, agent payments). NOT for custom model training, Rekognition, or Comprehend.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.pi/agent/skills/amazon-bedrock/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>aws-ai-ml</name>\n    <description>Selects, deploys, and customizes AI models on Amazon SageMaker. Fine-tuning (SFT, DPO, RLVR, RLAIF), model selection, dataset preparation, evaluation, deployment to SageMaker endpoints or Bedrock, and endpoint diagnostics. Covers the full lifecycle from planning through production. Use when fine-tuning models on SageMaker, choosing/selecting which base model to customize or fine-tune from SageMaker Hub, finding a model to deploy without fine-tuning, transforming datasets for training, checking data readiness, evaluating model quality, deploying to endpoints, setting up IAM roles and S3 buckets for training jobs, or managing a SageMaker Managed MLflow app. Also use to check endpoint health, diagnose failures, debug latency or errors, or view container logs and CloudWatch metrics. Covers Serverless Model Customization, Nova and OSS deployment paths, and PySDK v3 usage. NOT for Ground Truth labeling, Feature Store, or general-purpose AWS infrastructure.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.pi/agent/skills/aws-ai-ml/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>aws-auth</name>\n    <description>Adds user authentication to web and mobile apps with Amazon Cognito (user pools and identity pools) and the AWS Amplify client auth libraries. Covers sign-up/sign-in flows and the login page (Cognito-hosted UI / managed login), MFA, password policies, OAuth 2.0 / OIDC flows (auth-code + PKCE, client credentials), social/SAML federation, tokens (ID/access/refresh, rotation, revocation, storage), Cognito Lambda triggers, identity pools (temp AWS creds), and gating API Gateway (or ALB) routes to signed-in users via Cognito/JWT authorizers. Applies when adding a login or sign-up page, configuring a user pool or app client, choosing user pool vs identity pool, wiring social/SAML, refreshing tokens, requiring sign-in on an API Gateway or ALB, or debugging redirect_uri/token/MFA/CORS/federation errors. Does NOT cover Amplify Gen2 backend definitions (defineAuth, npx ampx → aws-amplify), IAM/STS/Identity Center (→ aws-iam), or API Gateway/Lambda resource config beyond the authorizer (→ aws-serverless).</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.pi/agent/skills/aws-auth/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>aws-billing-and-cost-management</name>\n    <description>Analyze AWS costs, find savings, manage budgets, evaluate Savings Plans and Reserved Instances, right-size EC2/Lambda/RDS/EBS with Compute Optimizer, look up service pricing, query CUR with Athena, detect cost anomalies, scope costs to billing views, and monitor Free Tier usage. Triggers on: AWS bill, cost analysis, reduce spend, savings plan, reserved instance, right-size, budget alert, cost optimization, pricing, free tier, cost anomaly, CUR, cost audit, billing view, billing view ARN.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.pi/agent/skills/aws-billing-and-cost-management/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>aws-blocks</name>\n    <description>Guides building full-stack applications with AWS Blocks — an Infrastructure-from-Code framework. Applies when creating APIs, selecting Building Blocks (KVStore, DistributedTable, Database, AuthBasic, AuthCognito, Realtime, AsyncJob, FileBucket, etc.), running local development, or deploying AWS Blocks applications. Also covers AWS Blocks topics with validated, version-specific patterns that prevent common mistakes. Triggers when user mentions AWS Blocks; project has aws-blocks/ directory; code imports @aws-blocks packages.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.pi/agent/skills/aws-blocks/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>aws-cdk</name>\n    <description>Authors, deploys, and troubleshoots AWS infrastructure using CDK with TypeScript or Python. Covers best practices, stack architecture, and construct patterns. Applies when writing CDK constructs, bootstrapping environments, running cdk deploy/synth/diff, fixing CDK or CloudFormation errors, planning stack structure, importing existing resources, resolving drift, or refactoring stacks without resource replacement.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.pi/agent/skills/aws-cdk/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>aws-cloudformation</name>\n    <description>Authors, validates, and troubleshoots AWS CloudFormation templates. Covers 